1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They've been called the near-perfect food

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
and the miracle crop,

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
from the mighty soybean in all its forms

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
to the world's most widely consumed legume.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,000
That's enough to feed about 2 million people.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
They supply a plentiful source of protein,

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
and they also produce gas in more ways than one.

8
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
That's why our beans are so much better.

9
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now we're spilling them.

10
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Beans on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Beans.

12
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:46,001
The world produces nearly 500 billion pounds of them a year.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
That's an impressive 70 pounds for every man, woman, and child

14
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:54,000
on Earth.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
They're one of the world's oldest crops, a source of

16
00:01:04,001 --> 00:01:07,000
cholesterol-free protein, rich in fiber and complex

17
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:11,000
carbohydrates, low in fat, and packed with vitamins and

18
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:14,000
minerals. Hundreds of bean varieties

19
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:18,000
grow around the globe. The number one bean grown in the

20
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:23,000
U.S. is also our most versatile, the soybean.

21
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:26,000
American soybean growers annually harvest nearly 75

22
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:29,260
nearly 75 million acres of the bean,

23
00:01:29,261 --> 00:01:33,440
or around 180 billion pounds.

24
00:01:33,441 --> 00:01:36,860
Like other beans, soy beans come from a family of vegetables

25
00:01:36,861 --> 00:01:40,500
called legumes and are harvested for the seeds, or beans,

26
00:01:40,501 --> 00:01:44,240
inside the pods.

27
00:01:44,241 --> 00:01:48,300
Sure, you can turn soy into soy sauce and tofu,

28
00:01:48,301 --> 00:01:50,700
but soy foods don't stop there.

29
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:52,900
How about hot dogs and burgers?

30
00:01:52,901 --> 00:01:54,300
Want cheese with that?

31
00:01:54,301 --> 00:01:55,360
More soy.

32
00:01:55,361 --> 00:01:56,820
Don't like burgers?

33
00:01:56,821 --> 00:01:59,320
How about chicken or meatballs?

34
00:01:59,321 --> 00:02:01,920
And for dessert, soy ice cream.

35
00:02:01,921 --> 00:02:03,520
Still, much of the soybeans' value

36
00:02:03,521 --> 00:02:05,260
lies in the oil it produces.

37
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:11,660
Surprisingly, almost 80% of all edible fats and oils

38
00:02:11,661 --> 00:02:16,120
consumed in the US come from soybean oil.

39
00:02:16,121 --> 00:02:17,900
This is soybean salad oil.

40
00:02:17,901 --> 00:02:22,720
You find it in salad dressings, mayonases, frying oils.

41
00:02:22,721 --> 00:02:25,980
This is an example of hydrogenated soybean oil.

42
00:02:25,981 --> 00:02:28,860
You would find this in a number of applications,

43
00:02:28,861 --> 00:02:34,620
including shortenings, non-dairy coffee creamers, cookies.

44
00:02:34,621 --> 00:02:38,860
If you put these two oils together, margarine.

45
00:02:38,861 --> 00:02:43,320
Before that oil ends in the frying pan or in your Twinkie,

46
00:02:43,321 --> 00:02:47,380
it must first be extracted from the soybean.

47
00:02:47,381 --> 00:02:50,480
The CHS oilseed processing plants in Fairmont

48
00:02:50,481 --> 00:02:54,320
Minnesota, operate around the clock to process more than 5

49
00:02:54,321 --> 00:02:59,120
billion pounds of soybeans each year, which results in 1 billion

50
00:02:59,121 --> 00:03:01,320
pounds of soybean oil.

51
00:03:01,321 --> 00:03:03,820
Our trucks are able to come in and deliver the soybeans

52
00:03:03,821 --> 00:03:07,240
directly from the fields 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

53
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:11,740
We average 300 truckloads of soybeans delivered per day.

54
00:03:11,741 --> 00:03:14,780
Soybeans ready for processing start at the top of this five

55
00:03:14,781 --> 00:03:19,240
story soybean crushing facility.

56
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:22,240
The beans are continuously fed into the top of this machine

57
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:25,780
where a series of screens located at different levels

58
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:27,680
do the separating.

59
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:30,080
This is where the field material is removed

60
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:32,180
from the mainstream of the beans.

61
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:35,280
After they pass out through this machine,

62
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:38,100
they're partially clean.

63
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:40,800
On the fourth floor, machines quickly heat the soybeans

64
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:44,900
to 170 degrees Fahrenheit.

65
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,300
Right now, the beans are in their whole form.

66
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:48,600
They're very hot.

67
00:03:48,601 --> 00:03:51,600
They're hot. They're 170 degrees.

68
00:03:51,601 --> 00:03:55,600
The reason why this is done is to provide an easier separation

69
00:03:55,601 --> 00:03:57,600
between the outer covering or the hull of the bean

70
00:03:57,601 --> 00:04:00,600
from the inner portions of the bean.

71
00:04:00,601 --> 00:04:03,600
This separation allows the hull of the soybean

72
00:04:03,601 --> 00:04:06,600
to be easily removed when the soybeans reach the grinders

73
00:04:06,601 --> 00:04:09,600
on the third floor.

74
00:04:09,601 --> 00:04:13,600
Here's an example of what's coming through this machine.

75
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:16,600
Here you see the soybean pieces with some of the hulls

76
00:04:16,601 --> 00:04:18,640
that have been removed.

77
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:20,700
One level down.

78
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:24,440
Large rotating cylinders, referred to as flaking mills,

79
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:27,640
flatten the crushed soybeans into dime-sized flakes

80
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:30,240
to increase their surface area.

81
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:33,080
Increasing the surface area allows

82
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:35,080
the greatest percentage of the soybean oil

83
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:37,980
to be extracted from the soybean.

84
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:40,260
You can physically grab these flakes

85
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:44,120
and feel the oil as it's being pressed in the surface.

86
00:04:44,121 --> 00:04:48,160
The flattened flakes now head for extraction.

87
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:52,360
The solvent hexane washes over the flakes, chemically bonds

88
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:55,960
with the oil, and separates it from the flakes.

89
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:58,200
The process is repeated multiple times

90
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:01,200
to extract the greatest amount of oil.

91
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:03,880
After extraction, heat evaporates the hexane

92
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:06,480
from the soybean oil.

93
00:05:06,580 --> 00:05:08,180
There's plenty of value left in the flakes

94
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:10,120
after the oil is gone.

95
00:05:10,220 --> 00:05:13,980
CHS grinds over 80% of the now defatted flakes

96
00:05:13,981 --> 00:05:19,280
into soybean meal, widely used in the animal feed industry.

97
00:05:19,281 --> 00:05:22,480
But the soybean oil isn't ready to eat yet.

98
00:05:22,481 --> 00:05:24,960
Every day, semis transport over 1 and 1

99
00:05:24,961 --> 00:05:28,820
half million pounds of it to the CHS plant in Mankato,

100
00:05:28,821 --> 00:05:31,340
50 miles away.

101
00:05:31,341 --> 00:05:34,640
We receive all the crude oil that is processed

102
00:05:34,641 --> 00:05:36,800
at Fairmont, Minnesota.

103
00:05:36,801 --> 00:05:40,600
And we receive approximately 36 truckloads a day

104
00:05:40,601 --> 00:05:42,080
of their production oil.

105
00:05:42,081 --> 00:05:45,080
The crude oil is unloaded in all these tanks,

106
00:05:45,081 --> 00:05:48,980
and we can store roughly 7 million pounds of raw crude oil

107
00:05:48,981 --> 00:05:50,480
in our plant.

108
00:05:50,481 --> 00:05:52,320
The plant pumps crude soybean oil

109
00:05:52,321 --> 00:05:55,460
through a secondary refining process, which washes

110
00:05:55,461 --> 00:05:59,460
and removes impurities from the oil, converting it into a form

111
00:05:59,461 --> 00:06:02,300
suitable for consumption.

112
00:06:02,301 --> 00:06:05,360
Right now, we're loading finished soybean oil

113
00:06:05,361 --> 00:06:06,580
into a truck.

114
00:06:06,581 --> 00:06:08,640
We load over 3 million pounds a day

115
00:06:08,641 --> 00:06:11,140
into trucks and rail cars for delivery

116
00:06:11,141 --> 00:06:13,300
throughout the world.

117
00:06:13,301 --> 00:06:15,040
Two additional soybean commodities

118
00:06:15,041 --> 00:06:16,780
are produced by CHS.

119
00:06:16,781 --> 00:06:20,820
Lecithin is a byproduct of the refining process.

120
00:06:20,821 --> 00:06:22,120
This is lecithin.

121
00:06:22,121 --> 00:06:25,080
Its most common use is as an emulsifier,

122
00:06:25,081 --> 00:06:27,720
which means it just holds things together.

123
00:06:27,721 --> 00:06:30,660
You'll find it in cosmetics, cooking sprays,

124
00:06:30,661 --> 00:06:32,500
and read your candy bar wrapper.

125
00:06:32,501 --> 00:06:35,840
You're gonna probably see it there.

126
00:06:35,841 --> 00:06:38,200
The other is soy flour.

127
00:06:38,201 --> 00:06:39,900
If you go to a grocery store right now

128
00:06:39,901 --> 00:06:42,400
you buy bread, most of the bread in the United States

129
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:45,500
has defatted soy flour in it.

130
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:47,600
Made from the remaining defatted flakes

131
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:52,380
and ground into a powder, soy flour acts as a protein source

132
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:54,220
and natural preservative in foods,

133
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:59,280
including pancake mixes, pastas, donuts, breads,

134
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:00,720
and pet food.

135
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:03,460
Vegetarian and vegan markets also use soy flour

136
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:05,920
as a source of protein.

137
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:09,100
The soy flour gets produced into a product called

138
00:07:09,101 --> 00:07:12,940
called textured soy protein, and that is used in a lot of

139
00:07:12,941 --> 00:07:16,640
applications where you can replace meat.

140
00:07:16,641 --> 00:07:18,540
While the creation of soy-based meat

141
00:07:18,541 --> 00:07:22,540
substitutes is relatively new, the soybean itself has been

142
00:07:22,541 --> 00:07:25,740
feeding families and farm animals since its cultivation

143
00:07:25,741 --> 00:07:30,860
began in China more than 3,000 years ago.

144
00:07:30,861 --> 00:07:34,460
One of the earliest references of soybean use in the US comes

145
00:07:34,461 --> 00:07:36,400
from the Civil War.

146
00:07:36,401 --> 00:07:39,340
When coffee beans were scarce, soldiers ground up dried

147
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:42,340
soybeans, referred to as coffee berries instead.

148
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:48,340
Thank American chemist George Washington Carver

149
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:52,940
for first identifying many of soybeans' uses.

150
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:55,160
Known for his research with the peanut,

151
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:58,360
Carver also experimented with the soybean.

152
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:00,500
From mayonnaise to paint, Carver

153
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:03,260
developed over 100 edible and industrial uses

154
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:04,160
from the soybean.

155
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:09,800
Long before Carver, the Chinese ground up soybeans

156
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:13,240
and mixed them with water to create soy milk.

157
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:18,140
It's been around in China since at least the first century AD.

158
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:19,880
But soy milk production in the US

159
00:08:19,980 --> 00:08:23,580
didn't start until the early 20th century.

160
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:27,780
In the 1990s, a large number of lactose intolerant people

161
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:30,920
and others wanting to get away from dairy products

162
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:33,400
helped soy milk jump from natural food stores

163
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:36,400
to supermarket shelves.

164
00:08:36,401 --> 00:08:41,300
Today, nearly 35% of the entire soy food market goes to soy milk.

165
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:47,940
One company enjoying soy milk's rise in popularity is Zen Soy.

166
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:51,520
A New Jersey-based company blends and packages soy milk products

167
00:08:51,521 --> 00:08:56,340
with a 12-month shelf life, refrigerated soy milk

168
00:08:56,341 --> 00:09:00,400
with a 90- to 100-day shelf life, and a line of soy puddings.

169
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:05,020
Creating a soy base starts with whole soybeans and hot water.

170
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:10,300
The mixture is ground into soy slurry.

171
00:09:10,301 --> 00:09:13,200
The slurry enters a high-speed centrifuge, which

172
00:09:13,201 --> 00:09:17,300
filters out the okara, or fiber.

173
00:09:17,301 --> 00:09:19,840
The soy liquid then receives an injection of high temperature

174
00:09:19,841 --> 00:09:23,760
steam to decontaminate it.

175
00:09:23,761 --> 00:09:27,600
It's then pumped inside a vacuum vessel, where it's flash-cooled

176
00:09:27,601 --> 00:09:32,200
to remove undesired bean-like flavors.

177
00:09:32,201 --> 00:09:35,500
This is where we receive our liquid bulk shipments

178
00:09:35,501 --> 00:09:37,500
In the course of a 24-hour period,

179
00:09:37,501 --> 00:09:40,500
we can receive 1.1 million pounds of soy base,

180
00:09:40,501 --> 00:09:43,500
which equates to 130,000 gallons.

181
00:09:43,501 --> 00:09:47,500
From here, it goes to our blending area.

182
00:09:47,501 --> 00:09:50,500
In the blending room, Zinsoy first adds ingredients

183
00:09:50,501 --> 00:09:53,500
to approximate the look and taste of cow's milk.

184
00:09:53,501 --> 00:09:56,500
Today's batch includes cocoa powder

185
00:09:56,501 --> 00:09:59,500
for Zinsoy's chocolate soy milk.

186
00:09:59,501 --> 00:10:01,500
Right now, we're at the start of the blending process,

187
00:10:01,501 --> 00:10:04,500
where the blender is adding the dry ingredients

188
00:10:04,501 --> 00:10:06,660
ingredients to the soy base.

189
00:10:06,661 --> 00:10:08,540
Right now he's adding the sugar.

190
00:10:08,541 --> 00:10:11,940
We add our stabilizers, flavor, and cocoa

191
00:10:11,941 --> 00:10:15,440
when we make the chocolate.

192
00:10:15,441 --> 00:10:17,540
Just like cow's milk, it has to go through

193
00:10:17,541 --> 00:10:19,340
ultra-pasteurization.

194
00:10:19,341 --> 00:10:21,860
The process involves holding the product

195
00:10:21,861 --> 00:10:25,420
at a specified temperature for a specified amount of time

196
00:10:25,421 --> 00:10:28,760
to kill any microbial growth in the product.

197
00:10:28,761 --> 00:10:34,500
We can pasteurize at 90 gallons a minute on this system.

198
00:10:34,501 --> 00:10:38,000
After pasteurization, achieving soy milk's extended shelf life

199
00:10:38,100 --> 00:10:41,200
comes down to container sterilization.

200
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:43,840
Today, we're packaging the Zen soy

201
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:47,540
into a refrigerated extended shelf life half gallon

202
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:50,840
container where we can achieve 90 to 100 days

203
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:54,220
of refrigerated shelf life compared to two weeks

204
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:56,860
on a standard milk filler.

205
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:58,760
The filler sterilizes each container

206
00:10:58,860 --> 00:11:04,500
with hydrogen peroxide and a dose of ultraviolet light.

207
00:11:04,501 --> 00:11:09,500
Then a blast of 285-degree heat evaporates the hydrogen peroxide

208
00:11:09,501 --> 00:11:13,500
solution before each carton is filled with soy milk.

209
00:11:13,501 --> 00:11:17,500
This specific filler can produce 9,000 units an hour.

210
00:11:17,501 --> 00:11:23,500
On a typical 24-hour run, that's 90,000 gallons of soy milk.

211
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:28,500
That's just part of the more than 200 million gallons

212
00:11:28,501 --> 00:11:32,500
of soy beverages Americans consume each year.

213
00:11:32,501 --> 00:11:35,840
Research suggests that soy milk and other soy foods

214
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:38,700
can lower the risk of heart disease, certain cancers,

215
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:40,340
and osteoporosis.

216
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:45,240
But other research indicates too much soy can be harmful.

217
00:11:45,340 --> 00:11:48,440
Soybeans contain a class of phytoestrogens called

218
00:11:48,540 --> 00:11:53,660
isoflavones, an estrogen-like chemical found in plants.

219
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:55,760
Studies suggest eating too much soy

220
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:59,800
can reduce the effects of naturally produced estrogen

221
00:11:59,801 --> 00:12:03,600
and cause menstrual irregularity in women,

222
00:12:03,601 --> 00:12:07,600
and may lower the sperm count in men.

223
00:12:07,601 --> 00:12:11,680
Of course, soybeans offer a lot more than sustenance,

224
00:12:11,681 --> 00:12:15,680
from biofuels, adhesives, inks, and crayons,

225
00:12:15,681 --> 00:12:20,180
to insulation, lubricants, foams, and plastics.

226
00:12:20,181 --> 00:12:25,000
It's no wonder the soybean has been called the miracle crop.

227
00:12:25,001 --> 00:12:27,800
Both carver and auto-industrialist Henry Ford

228
00:12:27,801 --> 00:12:29,640
Henry Ford recognized the soybean

229
00:12:29,641 --> 00:12:34,300
as a viable source for plant-based plastics and biofuel.

230
00:12:34,301 --> 00:12:37,640
In 1941, Ford attached a soy-based plastic trunk lid

231
00:12:37,641 --> 00:12:40,640
to his car and demonstrated its durability

232
00:12:40,641 --> 00:12:45,560
for reporters with an ax.

233
00:12:45,561 --> 00:12:50,460
62 years later, in 2003, agricultural equipment maker

234
00:12:50,461 --> 00:12:53,960
Case IH began replacing exterior steel panels

235
00:12:53,961 --> 00:12:57,640
on their 18-ton combines with a soybean-based plastic

236
00:12:57,641 --> 00:12:59,640
plastic composite.

237
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:01,880
Essentially what we're providing with the soybean

238
00:13:01,980 --> 00:13:04,580
base panels is a complete circle.

239
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:07,680
We take the crop out of the field,

240
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:10,680
it goes through a manufacturing process,

241
00:13:10,780 --> 00:13:14,320
becomes a trim panel for one of our case IH combines,

242
00:13:14,420 --> 00:13:17,200
and then that case IH combine is back in the field

243
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:19,200
harvesting the same crop again.

244
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:22,360
We're utilizing one of the very important commodities

245
00:13:22,460 --> 00:13:26,840
that our customers grow and produce.

246
00:13:26,841 --> 00:13:29,840
Although only a 3% savings in weight over its steel

247
00:13:29,841 --> 00:13:33,940
equivalent, the formability and cost of soybean-based plastic

248
00:13:33,941 --> 00:13:36,940
panels make them an ideal alternative for companies

249
00:13:36,941 --> 00:13:39,280
like Case IH.

250
00:13:39,281 --> 00:13:43,280
The molding process is much more precise than what we can

251
00:13:43,281 --> 00:13:46,200
reproduce with steel manufacturing.

252
00:13:46,201 --> 00:13:50,180
The molds utilized to manufacture the soybean-based

253
00:13:50,181 --> 00:13:55,900
composite panels are much less costly than if we were doing a

254
00:13:55,901 --> 00:14:00,400
system for steel components of comparable configuration

255
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:02,300
and shape.

256
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:04,400
The farmers who drive these red behemoths

257
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:08,240
season after season, harvest after harvest,

258
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:11,040
want a ruggedness comparable to steel.

259
00:14:11,140 --> 00:14:14,560
One of the advantages of the Case IH trim panels

260
00:14:14,660 --> 00:14:16,260
is certainly durability.

261
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:20,460
I'd like to demonstrate that for you just as Henry Ford

262
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:21,900
did some time ago.

263
00:14:25,900 --> 00:14:29,700
As you can see where the bowling ball did hit,

264
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:33,240
there's no physical damage to the trim panel.

265
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:37,020
But soybean use doesn't stop at the plastic panels.

266
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:39,440
This bean can also provide power.

267
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:42,280
Not only are K-Sai's combines produced

268
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:46,320
with soy-based paneling for the exterior of the combine,

269
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:51,560
they're also certified for the use of soy-based biofuels.

270
00:14:51,660 --> 00:14:54,200
Today's soy biodiesel is one of the fastest-growing

271
00:14:54,201 --> 00:14:56,840
of the fastest growing renewable fuels,

272
00:14:56,940 --> 00:15:00,900
with 600 million gallons produced in 2008.

273
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:05,280
All those edible products and all those industrial uses

274
00:15:05,380 --> 00:15:08,680
can come from the simple soybean.

275
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:11,820
Soybeans aren't the only beans on the bush.

276
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:14,460
There's another class called the dry bean.

277
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:16,460
Chances are you've eaten your share.

278
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:22,920
It takes just one acre of land to produce 356 pounds

279
00:15:22,921 --> 00:15:24,920
protein.

280
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:28,420
It takes 18 acres of land to produce the same amount of beef

281
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:29,200
protein.

282
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:37,960
In the universe of beans, soy may be the king of versatility,

283
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:42,340
becoming foods and non-food products that belie its true

284
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:43,880
nature.

285
00:15:43,940 --> 00:15:47,480
But there's another type of bean that most often stays a bean.

286
00:15:47,540 --> 00:15:51,520
It ends up on our salads, in our soups and chilies, and alongside

287
00:15:51,521 --> 00:15:55,220
and alongside our hot dogs, the dry bean.

288
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:57,520
According to the US Dry Bean Council,

289
00:15:57,620 --> 00:16:00,860
there are 14 major dry bean classes.

290
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:03,360
Like other legumes, beans have the ability

291
00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:07,360
to pull nitrogen from the air and transport it to the soil,

292
00:16:07,460 --> 00:16:10,580
making them ideal for rotation with nutrient-depleting crops.

293
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:16,280
For centuries, cultures around the world

294
00:16:16,380 --> 00:16:18,920
have valued dry beans not only for their low cost

295
00:16:18,921 --> 00:16:22,520
content, but because they're edible even after long periods

296
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:25,220
of storage.

297
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:28,500
Storability also made them ideal rations for sailors spending

298
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:31,000
months at sea.

299
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:34,800
Since the mid-1800s, the prevalence of this small white

300
00:16:34,900 --> 00:16:38,280
bean on US warships earned it an additional moniker,

301
00:16:38,380 --> 00:16:39,480
the navy bean.

302
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:47,740
Today, they're most commonly found in cans of baked beans.

303
00:16:47,741 --> 00:16:51,040
Since the 1920s, this brick building positioned

304
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:53,340
beside Casco Bay in Portland, Maine,

305
00:16:53,440 --> 00:16:56,940
has been producing B&M baked beans with ingredients

306
00:16:57,040 --> 00:16:59,380
and implements they consider to be part of a New England

307
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:01,480
tradition.

308
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:04,960
Sweet molasses, 200-pound steel pots,

309
00:17:05,060 --> 00:17:07,800
and fiery red brick ovens.

310
00:17:07,900 --> 00:17:11,400
They started with seven of these brick ovens in the early

311
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:15,140
20s, and then it grew to 14, and then 28.

312
00:17:15,141 --> 00:17:19,140
and currently we have 76 brick ovens that we bake our beans in.

313
00:17:19,141 --> 00:17:22,140
And it's done very much the same way it was done

314
00:17:22,141 --> 00:17:23,140
when they originally started.

315
00:17:23,141 --> 00:17:25,140
The process has never changed.

316
00:17:25,141 --> 00:17:28,140
Turning the small white beans into golden brown nuggets

317
00:17:28,141 --> 00:17:32,140
begins with a wash through the riffle machine.

318
00:17:32,141 --> 00:17:35,140
In these riffles, we take out any foreign material,

319
00:17:35,141 --> 00:17:38,140
whether it's rocks, corn, or anything will get caught

320
00:17:38,141 --> 00:17:40,140
in these riffles.

321
00:17:40,141 --> 00:17:44,140
And then it drops into this tank where they're pumped over

322
00:17:44,141 --> 00:17:46,380
into the blancher.

323
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:48,140
Rather than boiling the beans,

324
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:50,380
the blancher acts like a giant steamer.

325
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:54,380
Inside, the temperature reaches 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

326
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:56,580
The purpose of this is to blanch the beans

327
00:17:56,680 --> 00:17:58,980
so the beans can take in moisture,

328
00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:00,580
take in more water.

329
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:03,500
And so once they come out of this steamer,

330
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:05,500
they can go downstairs, and they'll

331
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:07,500
make it a lot easier for the molasses

332
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:11,900
to be absorbed into the beans in the next part of the process.

333
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,080
One floor down, workers need to

334
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:18,140
up each pot with 250 pounds of blanched beans.

335
00:18:18,141 --> 00:18:21,140
Then position them under the sauce station.

336
00:18:21,141 --> 00:18:25,140
Over here is your sauce, which is made out of molasses

337
00:18:25,141 --> 00:18:27,140
and sugar and water.

338
00:18:27,141 --> 00:18:30,140
And then over here, you'll have a mustard slurry come out.

339
00:18:30,141 --> 00:18:32,140
And this is pork slurry.

340
00:18:35,140 --> 00:18:37,140
And we wait for the liquid to all drain,

341
00:18:37,141 --> 00:18:39,140
and then we give it a good stir.

342
00:18:39,141 --> 00:18:43,140
I do anywhere from 100 to 130 of these a day.

343
00:18:44,140 --> 00:18:47,540
Soaked with sauce, each pot of beans heads to one of B&M's

344
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:50,640
76 brick ovens.

345
00:18:50,740 --> 00:18:52,880
Timing is crucial to the baked beans' taste,

346
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:54,780
and determining the right amount of heat

347
00:18:54,880 --> 00:18:57,280
is all about the bubble.

348
00:18:57,380 --> 00:18:59,780
These ovens are old enough so that they

349
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:02,300
have no thermostat on them.

350
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:05,000
The only way we can tell if it's the right temperature

351
00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:07,700
is to watch the bubble on the outside of the pot,

352
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:09,600
and we adjust the burners to that.

353
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:11,500
There's a nice little bubble all the way around.

354
00:19:11,600 --> 00:19:13,400
That's how we know we have the right amount of heat

355
00:19:13,401 --> 00:19:14,660
in these ovens.

356
00:19:14,760 --> 00:19:16,200
This is old-world technology.

357
00:19:16,300 --> 00:19:17,640
This is hands-on.

358
00:19:17,740 --> 00:19:22,200
We're true to the old-fashioned New England baked beans.

359
00:19:22,300 --> 00:19:25,540
We still do it the way they did it 150 years ago.

360
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:27,340
Nice steam coming up.

361
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:28,440
Oh, you can tell.

362
00:19:28,540 --> 00:19:31,560
Those beans right there are wicked good.

363
00:19:31,660 --> 00:19:34,060
Once baking is complete, each pot of beans

364
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:39,060
is hung over a metal trough and drained of sauce.

365
00:19:39,160 --> 00:19:41,700
Workers dump the pots down a steel chute,

366
00:19:41,701 --> 00:19:44,500
which leads the beans to the canning room.

367
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:47,200
Here, the drained sauce is added back to the beans

368
00:19:47,300 --> 00:19:51,880
as each can passes under the sauce waterfall station.

369
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:54,780
There, they get a second dose of sauce

370
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:57,280
to fill up the can and give it that distinctive New England

371
00:19:57,380 --> 00:19:58,220
flavor.

372
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:01,420
That's just an integral part of B&M beans.

373
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:06,920
During a typical day here, we'll

374
00:20:07,020 --> 00:20:09,320
do somewhere in the neighborhood of 9,000 cases,

375
00:20:09,321 --> 00:20:14,320
which is about 115,000 individual cans.

376
00:20:14,420 --> 00:20:17,220
The secret ingredient in our product is time.

377
00:20:17,320 --> 00:20:20,460
You have to give the bean time to absorb the sugars

378
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:23,260
and molasses flavors before you can.

379
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:29,080
The beans baked at B&M are just part of the 2.5 billion

380
00:20:29,180 --> 00:20:33,580
pounds of dry beans American farmers produce each year.

381
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:37,680
In the United States, about 40 states grow dry beans,

382
00:20:37,681 --> 00:20:42,080
But only about 18 states grow them for commercial operations.

383
00:20:42,180 --> 00:20:44,480
The top bean-producing state is North Dakota,

384
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:49,020
and they grow about 42% of the beans grown in the US.

385
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:51,520
The number one dry bean grown in the US

386
00:20:51,620 --> 00:20:56,440
is the pinto, occupying over 600,000 acres of farmland.

387
00:20:56,540 --> 00:21:00,800
That's about 1.1 billion pounds of beans.

388
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:03,240
The pinto bean, along with an assortment of dry beans

389
00:21:03,340 --> 00:21:07,180
we consume today, originated in South and Central American

390
00:21:07,181 --> 00:21:08,680
regions.

391
00:21:08,780 --> 00:21:12,320
Some were domesticated over 7,000 years ago.

392
00:21:12,420 --> 00:21:14,680
As the cultivation of these beans spread throughout North

393
00:21:14,780 --> 00:21:18,920
America, so did the methods for growing them.

394
00:21:19,020 --> 00:21:21,420
Native American tribes taught early settlers a planting

395
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:24,940
technique they called the Three Sisters.

396
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:27,440
Beans were grown in combination with corn and squash.

397
00:21:30,040 --> 00:21:33,440
The corn provided stalks for the beans to climb.

398
00:21:33,540 --> 00:21:36,240
The prickly squash leaves deterred animals and provided

399
00:21:36,241 --> 00:21:40,240
to keep the soil moist and prevent weed growth.

400
00:21:40,340 --> 00:21:43,240
The bean plants supplied nitrogen to replenish the soil.

401
00:21:45,880 --> 00:21:48,980
Of course, commercial beans aren't grown that way today.

402
00:21:49,080 --> 00:21:50,980
Many dry beans pass through facilities

403
00:21:51,080 --> 00:21:53,300
like the Kelly Bean Company, headquartered

404
00:21:53,400 --> 00:21:55,100
in western Nebraska.

405
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:57,000
The family-owned and operated company

406
00:21:57,100 --> 00:22:00,300
has been in the bean business since 1927

407
00:22:00,400 --> 00:22:05,000
and since amassed one big hill of beans.

408
00:22:05,001 --> 00:22:09,040
Through our multiple plants in the western half of the United States,

409
00:22:09,041 --> 00:22:12,540
we originated about 12 different varieties of dry beans.

410
00:22:12,541 --> 00:22:15,720
So whether you're going to find pinto beans in your taco

411
00:22:15,721 --> 00:22:17,680
or great northern beans in your soup

412
00:22:17,681 --> 00:22:19,980
or navy beans in your can of baked beans,

413
00:22:19,981 --> 00:22:21,340
we handle all varieties of beans.

414
00:22:21,341 --> 00:22:22,860
Through all of our operations,

415
00:22:22,861 --> 00:22:26,820
we're running 250 million pounds of beans per year.

416
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:31,500
At Kelly Beans Mitchell, Nebraska facility,

417
00:22:31,501 --> 00:22:35,100
80,000 pounds of dry beans pass through the cleaning and sorting

418
00:22:35,101 --> 00:22:36,500
equipment every day.

419
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:43,500
The beans first pass through a series of screens to shake out

420
00:22:43,501 --> 00:22:46,500
farmland debris and beans that don't size up.

421
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:53,500
Next, gravity decides which beans make it to the customer.

422
00:22:53,501 --> 00:22:57,500
Here we are at the end of the gravity machine, the weight

423
00:22:57,501 --> 00:22:59,000
the weight machine.

424
00:22:59,001 --> 00:23:02,000
And what you can see is the lighter product that's

425
00:23:02,001 --> 00:23:06,000
coming off the deck is being separated from the good beans.

426
00:23:06,001 --> 00:23:08,740
This will go into livestock beans.

427
00:23:08,741 --> 00:23:12,540
The heavy product here will go into the bag.

428
00:23:12,541 --> 00:23:15,460
It is the finished product.

429
00:23:15,461 --> 00:23:17,160
All those dry beans in the middle

430
00:23:17,161 --> 00:23:20,160
enter the high-tech side of Kelly Bean's operation,

431
00:23:20,161 --> 00:23:22,500
the electric eyes.

432
00:23:22,501 --> 00:23:25,160
These speedy bean counters employ eight shoots,

433
00:23:25,161 --> 00:23:28,600
each handling 10 lanes of bean traffic.

434
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:30,000
Their job?

435
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:33,900
Search for and pluck out the bad beans based on color.

436
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:35,800
Today, the eyes are set up for white beans,

437
00:23:35,900 --> 00:23:38,800
like the Great Northern.

438
00:23:38,900 --> 00:23:42,820
What this machine does is it separates the white beans.

439
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:46,620
It takes the bad beans out so that the product can be a good

440
00:23:46,720 --> 00:23:49,160
bean when it goes to the consumer.

441
00:23:49,260 --> 00:23:54,020
I can run anywhere from 10,000 pounds to 12,000 pounds of beans

442
00:23:54,021 --> 00:23:55,020
per hour.

443
00:23:58,020 --> 00:24:01,020
And right here, you can see on the eye,

444
00:24:01,021 --> 00:24:04,860
there's the camera, that little square shot there,

445
00:24:04,861 --> 00:24:08,460
so that this machine can read the bean,

446
00:24:08,461 --> 00:24:11,280
read the color of the bean.

447
00:24:11,281 --> 00:24:14,080
As each bean passes in front of an electric eye,

448
00:24:14,081 --> 00:24:16,980
it's compared against a white background.

449
00:24:16,981 --> 00:24:19,020
I think we're about ready to fire it up.

450
00:24:19,021 --> 00:24:25,020
It's looking for blight, worm tube, frozen bean,

451
00:24:25,021 --> 00:24:28,020
and also a rust color on the bean.

452
00:24:33,020 --> 00:24:34,020
It reads that bean.

453
00:24:34,021 --> 00:24:37,020
It's only got a split second to get it out of there.

454
00:24:37,021 --> 00:24:40,020
Compressed air blows out any questionable beans

455
00:24:40,021 --> 00:24:42,020
within the first 30 lanes.

456
00:24:42,021 --> 00:24:45,020
These beans go back to the top of the machine

457
00:24:45,021 --> 00:24:47,020
and make another pass through the next 30 lanes

458
00:24:47,021 --> 00:24:49,520
through the next 30 lanes to ensure none of the good beans

459
00:24:49,521 --> 00:24:52,160
were missed.

460
00:24:52,161 --> 00:24:54,620
Any rejects in these lanes get one final pass

461
00:24:54,621 --> 00:24:57,660
through the last 20.

462
00:24:57,661 --> 00:25:00,160
The main thing of it is what we're trying to do

463
00:25:00,161 --> 00:25:02,360
is get a good product.

464
00:25:02,361 --> 00:25:06,380
That's what goes to the customer right there.

465
00:25:06,381 --> 00:25:08,380
Each one-ton tote of good beans

466
00:25:08,381 --> 00:25:11,380
ends up at Kelly's 400,000-square-foot packaging

467
00:25:11,381 --> 00:25:13,880
and storage facility in Morrill, Nebraska.

468
00:25:17,020 --> 00:25:20,860
We're going to package about 200,000 pounds of beans today.

469
00:25:20,861 --> 00:25:24,020
That's enough to feed about 2 million people.

470
00:25:24,021 --> 00:25:26,700
Kelly's beans end up on grocery store shelves,

471
00:25:26,701 --> 00:25:29,200
in the food service industry, and are exported

472
00:25:29,201 --> 00:25:33,000
to more than 20 countries.

473
00:25:33,001 --> 00:25:35,700
Even though dry beans claim the number one bean eaten

474
00:25:35,701 --> 00:25:39,380
in America with the pinto, it still isn't the most widely

475
00:25:39,381 --> 00:25:41,780
consumed bean in the world.

476
00:25:41,781 --> 00:25:43,640
What is it?

477
00:25:43,641 --> 00:25:44,380
Here's a hint.

478
00:25:47,020 --> 00:25:52,020
The British devour 500 million cans of baked beans every year,

479
00:25:52,021 --> 00:25:55,020
making them the world's largest consumer.

480
00:25:55,021 --> 00:25:58,020
That's 16 cans of baked beans per Brit per year.

481
00:26:01,020 --> 00:26:04,020
In Italy, it's called the ceci.

482
00:26:04,021 --> 00:26:08,020
The Spanish named it the garbanzo.

483
00:26:08,021 --> 00:26:12,020
Other cultures know it as the chickpea.

484
00:26:12,021 --> 00:26:15,020
Whatever you call it, more than 18 and a half billion pounds

485
00:26:15,021 --> 00:26:19,820
of this bean produced a year make it the most widely consumed

486
00:26:19,821 --> 00:26:21,020
legume in the world.

487
00:26:23,820 --> 00:26:28,460
Over 65% of the planet's chickpeas come from India.

488
00:26:28,461 --> 00:26:31,500
But it's their use in hummus, a popular dish originating

489
00:26:31,501 --> 00:26:34,140
from Middle Eastern and Mediterranean cultures,

490
00:26:34,141 --> 00:26:38,140
that contribute to the chickpeas' global dominance.

491
00:26:38,141 --> 00:26:41,820
Hummus is conquering America, too, where it's commonly eaten

492
00:26:41,821 --> 00:26:44,140
as a condiment or dip.

493
00:26:44,141 --> 00:26:47,980
Tribe Mediterranean Foods has seen its hummus sales

494
00:26:48,080 --> 00:26:51,440
skyrocket thanks to a more health-conscious and diverse

495
00:26:51,540 --> 00:26:55,420
population and large vegetarian following.

496
00:26:55,520 --> 00:26:59,380
Tribe Mediterranean started producing hummus back in 1993

497
00:26:59,480 --> 00:27:02,300
when the hummus category was very, very tiny in the whole

498
00:27:02,400 --> 00:27:05,420
U.S. Across the country, we probably did maybe $5 million

499
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:07,260
in total retail sales.

500
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:10,800
Today's number is well over $180 million in retail sales,

501
00:27:10,900 --> 00:27:13,140
so the escalation of the popularity of the product

502
00:27:13,141 --> 00:27:15,980
The popularity of the product has grown tremendously

503
00:27:15,981 --> 00:27:17,980
in the past several years.

504
00:27:17,981 --> 00:27:20,840
In order to turn the 200,000 pounds of chickpeas

505
00:27:20,841 --> 00:27:23,840
it receives every week into hummus,

506
00:27:23,841 --> 00:27:27,740
Tribe must first give the dry beans a good soaking,

507
00:27:27,741 --> 00:27:29,940
10 tons at a time.

508
00:27:29,941 --> 00:27:32,640
In this area, we hydrate and clean the pea.

509
00:27:32,641 --> 00:27:36,340
We have one silo that we hydrate the peas overnight.

510
00:27:36,341 --> 00:27:40,240
The other silo is for processed peas during the day.

511
00:27:40,241 --> 00:27:41,940
We'll hydrate them.

512
00:27:41,941 --> 00:27:43,740
They'll come down the flume.

513
00:27:43,741 --> 00:27:51,540
They'll expand 3 1⁄2 times their weight as we clean them.

514
00:27:51,541 --> 00:27:54,780
After the wash water is separated from the chickpeas,

515
00:27:54,781 --> 00:27:57,580
they enter one of two pressure kettles.

516
00:27:57,581 --> 00:28:03,500
Each kettle cooks 1,600 pounds of chickpeas within 15 minutes.

517
00:28:03,501 --> 00:28:06,140
In this area, we grind the peas after they're cooked.

518
00:28:06,141 --> 00:28:09,240
The kettles drop into an even feed conveyor.

519
00:28:09,241 --> 00:28:12,040
